DATA.lessons.push({id:"lesson-1",num:1,title:"Setup & Your First App",description:"Install Flutter and understand the skeleton every app is built on: main(), runApp(), MaterialApp, Scaffold.",markdown:"# Lesson 1: Setup & Your First App\n\n## What is Flutter?\n\nFlutter is Google's open-source toolkit for building apps from a **single codebase**. Write your code once in the Dart language, and Flutter compiles it into native apps for Android, iOS, web, and desktop.\n\nThe big idea behind Flutter is simple: **everything on screen is a widget**. Buttons, text, layouts, even the app itself — all widgets, composed together like Lego blocks. You'll see exactly what that means in Lesson 2.\n\n## What you need\n\n1. **Flutter SDK** — download it from flutter.dev and add it to your system PATH.\n2. **An editor** — VS Code with the Flutter extension is the lightest setup and works great.\n3. **Verify** — open a terminal and run `flutter doctor`. It tells you exactly what's missing.\n\nThat's it. No heavy IDE required to start learning.\n\n## The anatomy of a Flutter app\n\nEvery Flutter app follows the same skeleton. Open it up and you'll always find these four pieces:\n\n- **`main()`** — the entry point. Every Dart program starts here.\n- **`runApp()`** — takes your root widget and draws it on the screen.\n- **`MaterialApp`** — the root widget. It gives your app Material Design styling, navigation, and themes for free.\n- **`Scaffold`** — the basic page structure: an app bar on top, a body in the middle.\n\nHere is the smallest real Flutter app:\n\n```dart\nimport 'package:flutter/material.dart';\n\nvoid main() {\n  runApp(const MyApp());\n}\n\nclass MyApp extends StatelessWidget {\n  const MyApp({super.key});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return MaterialApp(\n      title: 'My First App',\n      home: Scaffold(\n        appBar: AppBar(\n          title: const Text('My First App'),\n        ),\n        body: const Center(\n          child: Text('Hello, Flutter!'),\n        ),\n      ),\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nRead it top to bottom: `main()` runs your app, `MaterialApp` sets it up, `Scaffold` builds the page, and inside the body a `Center` widget holds a `Text` widget saying hello. Widgets inside widgets inside widgets — that nesting is the whole game.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **main()** — where every Dart program begins execution.\n- **runApp()** — inflates the widget tree and attaches it to the screen.\n- **Widget** — the basic building block of a Flutter UI.\n- **BuildContext** — a handle to the widget's location in the tree (you'll use it constantly).\n\n## Try it yourself\n\nPaste the code above into DartPad (or your editor), press run, and change the text to your own name. Breaking things and fixing them is how this sticks — then take the lesson quiz to check what stuck.\n",pdf:"data:application/octet-stream;base64,",quizId:"quiz-1"});